`src/` is untouched: `git status --short src/` returns 0 lines.

## Findings fixed

**1 — BLOCKER (cycle test on real types + a leaking program that leaks).** The bespoke `Node { payload: u32 }` is gone. The cycle is now built from records a **real Redblue program** made (`CYCLE_SOURCE`), held in `std::sync::Arc` — what `Shared` aliases — and the leak is **measured in live bytes** via a counting global allocator. `edge_a_reference_cycle_is_never_reclaimed` asserts **46,138,670 bytes** still held after every handle outside the cycle is dropped, and **zero** released by the last one; `edge_a_redblue_program_hands_back_everything_it_allocated` runs the same program and asserts **4,692 bytes** residual. One measurement, opposite directions, so the leak is attributable to the cycle rather than described.

Representation-sensitivity is now enforced by four gates, plus `edge_writing_a_record_into_its_own_field_copies_instead_of_closing_a_cycle` (`Arc::ptr_eq` on real `Shared<Fields>`) and `a_redblue_value_has_no_interior_mutability_and_no_back_reference`.

**2 — MAJOR.** The override test now sets `REDBLUE_MAX_CALL_DEPTH`, and reads the limit back out of the error message for unset / `7` / `"  12  "` / `"not a number"` / `"0"` / `"-3"`, and exercises `Vm::with_max_call_depth` with the variable set and for a zero.

**3 — MAJOR.** Forbidden list extended with `::downgrade`, `::upgrade`, `strong_count`, `weak_count`, `ptr_eq`, plus a `Cargo.toml` scan for seven collector crates.

**4 — MAJOR.** `docs/MEMORY.md` now names both property-write sites, both `Value::Record` guards, and the silent fall-through; `a_property_write_reaches_a_record_and_no_program_can_make_an_object` pins it behaviourally (a property write on a number leaves it `5`; `object` binds a `Record`).

**5 — MINOR.** Citation split into `src/value.rs:85-91` (body), `:88-90` (the `Arc` rationale), `:93` (`captured: Arc<Captured>`), all pinned.

Every new gate was **mutation-tested** — each was broken on purpose and the suite watched it go red (details in `REPORT.md`). Notably, the exact hole the reviewer named for finding 3 (an inferred-type `Arc::downgrade` back-reference) passed the old test and fails the new one.

The one thing I could not deliver as literally asked: **no Redblue program can leak today.** That is a theorem, not a survey — a self-referential `Arc<Vec<Value>>` is unconstructible in safe Rust because a self-reference *is* a handle, so it can never be taken at strong count one. I proved it in `FINDINGS.md` and confirmed it empirically (7 candidate programs, all returning to 0 bytes), and `MEMORY.md` says so under its own heading rather than implying a leak that isn't there.
